( ) For an elastic collision of 2 masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ with initial velocities $u_1$ and $u_2$, final velocities of masses after collision are

v_1=\left(\frac{m_1-m_2}{m_1+m_2}\right) u_1+\frac{2 m_2 u_2}{\left(m_1+m_2\right)}

and

v_2=\left(\frac{m_2-m_1}{m_1+m_2}\right) u_2+\frac{2 m_1 u_2}{m_1+m_2}
$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FKqsBxBKaLEI5rn2ypEvydbq6op5qYrpQUn8Pdz56c34.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FKqsBxBKaLEI5rn2ypEvydbq6op5qYrpQUn8Pdz56c34.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FKqsBxBKaLEI5rn2ypEvydbq6op5qYrpQUn8Pdz56c34.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
These are obtained as standard results by applying conservation of energy and momentum equations. Now, in given case at first bullet is embedded in mass $M_1$. Let speed of bullet + Block system be $v_1$ after collision.$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252F2K_YzJe5tJsqgSp3Ic6TfGnL727d9vz1-7Ii6cWUUHY.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2F2K_YzJe5tJsqgSp3Ic6TfGnL727d9vz1-7Ii6cWUUHY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2F2K_YzJe5tJsqgSp3Ic6TfGnL727d9vz1-7Ii6cWUUHY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
Then by conservation of momentum we have.

\begin{aligned}
& m v=\left(m+M_1\right) v_1 \\
& \text { or } \quad v_1=\left(\frac{m v}{m+M_1}\right)
\end{aligned}

Note Here we are not using standard result as first collision is perfectly inelastic.
Now, bullet $+M_1$ strikes $M_2$ and it is given in problem that this collision is elastic.$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FHTaodU3BVpgswBNuCO9za94dRGZf3IuALEmeiAfXu8I.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FHTaodU3BVpgswBNuCO9za94dRGZf3IuALEmeiAfXu8I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FHTaodU3BVpgswBNuCO9za94dRGZf3IuALEmeiAfXu8I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
Now, by formula we have,

\begin{aligned}
v_2 & =\left(\frac{\left(m+M_1\right)-M_2}{m+M_1+M_2}\right) v_1+0 \\
\text { and, } v_3 & =\left(\frac{M_2-\left(m+M_1\right)}{M_2+m+M_1} \times 0\right)+\left(\frac{2\left(m+M_1\right) v_1}{m+M_1+M_2}\right) \\
\Rightarrow \quad v_3 & =\frac{2\left(m+M_1\right)}{m+M_1+M_2} \cdot \frac{m v}{\left(m+M_1\right)} \\
& =\frac{2 m v}{m+M_1+M_2}
\end{aligned}

Note You can remember these standard and useful results for finding final velocities in case of elastic collisions.

Mass of the buIlet $=m$
Speed of bullet $=v$
According to question, bullets gets embedded into the bag, then they will move with common velocity $v_1$ (say).
This is the case of perfectly enelastic collision.
$\therefore$ Initial kinetic energy of bullet,

K_i=\frac{1}{2} m v^2

By the conservation of linear momentum,

\Rightarrow \quad \begin{aligned}
m v & =(M+m) v_1 \\
\Rightarrow \quad & v_1=\frac{m v}{M+m}
\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Final kinetic energy, $K_f=\frac{1}{2}(M+m) v_1^2$
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{1}{2}(M+m) \frac{m^2 v^2}{(M+m)^2} \quad \text { [from Eq. (ii)] } \\
& =\frac{1}{2} \frac{m^2 v^2}{M+m}
\end{aligned}

Loss in kine tic energy $=K_i-K_f$
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{1}{2} m v^2-\frac{1}{2} \frac{m^2 v^2}{M+m} \\
& =\frac{1}{2} m v^2\left(1-\frac{m}{M+m}\right)=\frac{m M v^2}{2(M+m)}
\end{aligned}

Centre of mass of two atoms of oxygen is given as

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{CM}} & =\frac{m_1 \mathrm{r}_1+m_2 \mathrm{r}_2}{m_1+m_2}=\frac{m \mathrm{r}_1+m \mathrm{r}_2}{m+m}\left[\because m_1=m_2=m\right] \\
& =\frac{m\left(\mathrm{r}_1+\mathrm{r}_2\right)}{2 m} \\
\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{CM}} & =\frac{\mathrm{r}_1+\mathrm{r}_2}{2}
\end{aligned}

According to question, the given situation is shown in the following figure.

<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FHwJP1G15vC0UKhoLCnuaZF8ph0Q3m-vyb_Ksb6KatQ4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FHwJP1G15vC0UKhoLCnuaZF8ph0Q3m-vyb_Ksb6KatQ4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FHwJP1G15vC0UKhoLCnuaZF8ph0Q3m-vyb_Ksb6KatQ4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br>

Mass of block, $M=2 \mathrm{~kg}$
Mass of bullet, $m_1=0.01 \mathrm{~kg}$
Speed of buIlet, $v=500 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$
h=0.1 \mathrm{~m}

If $v_1$ and $v_2$ be the velocities of the bullet and blocks after collision, then by conservation of energy,

\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{2} M v_2^2 & =M g h \\
v_2 & =\sqrt{2 g h}=\sqrt{2 \times 9.8 \times 0.1}=1.4 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}
\end{aligned}

According to the law of conservation of momentum,

\begin{aligned}
m_1 v & =m_1 v_1+M v_2 \\
\Rightarrow \quad v_1 & =\frac{m_1 v-M v_2}{m_1}=\frac{0.01 \times 500-2 \times 1.4}{0.01} \\
& =\frac{5-28}{0.01}=\frac{2.2}{0.01}=220 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}
\end{aligned}

Given, mass of bomb, \(M=9 \mathrm{~kg}\)
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FwwsJHIuWWNu0FWe2g0jv77FwKis7yMejIqpcljkLjsI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FwwsJHIuWWNu0FWe2g0jv77FwKis7yMejIqpcljkLjsI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FwwsJHIuWWNu0FWe2g0jv77FwKis7yMejIqpcljkLjsI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br>
According to conservation of linear momentum,
\(\begin{aligned}
& M \times 0 & =m_1 v_1+m_2 v_2 \\
\Rightarrow & 9 \times 0 & =3 \times 16+6 \times v_2 \\
\Rightarrow & v_2 & =\frac{-3 \times 16}{6}=-8 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}\)
\(\therefore\) Kinetic energy of mass, \(m_2=6 \mathrm{~kg}\) (given)
\(\begin{aligned}
K & =\frac{1}{2} m_2 v_2^2=\frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times(-8)^2 \\
& =\frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times 64=192 \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}\)

Given, initial kinetic energy, \(K_1=K\)
Final kinetic energy, \(K_2=4 K\)
Momentum and kinetic energy are related as,
\(\begin{aligned}
& \qquad K=\frac{p^2}{2 m} \Rightarrow p=\sqrt{2 m K} \\
& \begin{aligned}
\therefore \text {Initial momentum, } p_1 & =\sqrt{2 m K_1} \\
\Rightarrow & =\sqrt{2 m K} \quad \ldots (i) \\
\text {Final momentum, } p_2 & =\sqrt{2 m K_2}=\sqrt{2 m \cdot 4 K} \\
\Rightarrow & \ldots \text { (i) } \\
& =2 \sqrt{2 m K}=2 p_1 \quad \text { [from Eq. (i)] }
\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}\)

Here, mass of the body, $m=2 \mathrm{~kg}$ and time,

t=4 \mathrm{~s}

Impulse $=p_f-p_i$
where, $p_i=$ initial momentum,
$p_f=$ final momentum

Impulse $\quad=m v_f-m v_i=m\left(v_f-v_i\right) \quad(\because p=m v)$
As from the graph,

v_f=\frac{3-3}{8-4}=0 \text { and } v_i=\frac{3-0}{4-0}=\frac{3}{4}

\text { Impulse }=2 \times\left(0-\frac{3}{4}\right)=-1.5 \mathrm{~kg}-\mathrm{ms}^{-1}

Hence, the correct option is (b).

The centre of mass of the spheres is given by

x_{\mathrm{CM}}=\frac{m_1 x_1+m_2 x_2+m_3 x_3}{m_1+m_2+m_3}
$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FFMyjmNtTzhm3ZL1TWw5qUvwJFct9C1dISbuSqYeEwN4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FFMyjmNtTzhm3ZL1TWw5qUvwJFct9C1dISbuSqYeEwN4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FFMyjmNtTzhm3ZL1TWw5qUvwJFct9C1dISbuSqYeEwN4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
As the spheres are identical,

\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad x_{\mathrm{CM}} & =\frac{m}{3 m}(0+2 \sqrt{3}+\sqrt{3}) \\
& =\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{3}=\sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}

Similarly,

\begin{aligned}
y_{\mathrm{CM}} & =\frac{m}{3 m}\left(y_1+y_2+y_3\right) \\
& =\frac{(0+0+3)}{3}=1
\end{aligned}

So, the centre of mass, $C_{\mathrm{CM}}=(\sqrt{3}, 1)$
If one sphere is removed (say $C$ ), then

\begin{aligned}
& x_{\mathrm{CM}}^{\prime}=\frac{0+2 \sqrt{3}}{2}=\sqrt{3} \\
& y_{\mathrm{CM}}^{\prime}=0
\end{aligned}
$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fap_eamcet%252FA-bIF7WVaBaiSu5Hk6ok8eZPeh3uRNpJMskx3bjbA7s.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FA-bIF7WVaBaiSu5Hk6ok8eZPeh3uRNpJMskx3bjbA7s.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fap_eamcet%2FA-bIF7WVaBaiSu5Hk6ok8eZPeh3uRNpJMskx3bjbA7s.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
So, $\quad C_{\mathrm{CM}}^{\prime}=(\sqrt{3}, 0)$
Hence, the centre of mass shifted by $1 \mathrm{~m}$ in $-y$ direction. The correct option is (b).

For a rocket,

m \frac{d v}{d t}=F_{\text {ext }}+v \frac{d m}{d t}

Without any external force $\left(F_{\text {ext }}=0\right)$,

\begin{array}{ll} 
& m \frac{d v}{d t}=v \frac{d m}{d t} \Rightarrow \int \frac{d v}{v}=\int \frac{d m}{m} \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \log v=\log m+C
\end{array}

When, $v=0, m=m_0$
\therefore \quad C=-\log m_0

So, we have

\frac{\Delta v}{v}=\log \frac{m}{m_0} \cdot d t \text { or } m=m_0 e^{-a t / v}

Let initially particles are at origin, their masses are $m$ and $2 m$.
